## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where Google Meet realtime audio bridges could accept malformed base64 audio chunks from direct `googlemeet.chrome` calls or paired-node `pullAudio` responses.

## Why This Change Was Made

The Google Meet audio bridge now uses one owner-local base64 validation helper before policy forwarding, node-host playback, realtime voice input, and default agent transcription input. Valid audio chunks still flow through unchanged.

## User Impact

Malformed bridge audio is rejected or counted toward the existing transient input failure cutoff instead of being decoded into arbitrary bytes and sent to playback, realtime voice, or agent transcription providers.

## Evidence

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/google-meet/src/node-invoke-policy.test.ts extensions/google-meet/node-host.test.ts extensions/google-meet/index.test.ts`: 161 tests passed.
- `node --import tsx --input-type=module -`: imported the real policy, realtime bridge, and default agent bridge modules, proving invalid direct `pushAudio` does not dispatch, invalid pulled audio does not reach either `provider.sendAudio` or `transcriptionSession.sendAudio`, repeated malformed pulled chunks stop at the existing 5-error cutoff, and valid control chunks still do.
- Actual node-host command-pair run of the production `handleGoogleMeetNodeHostCommand` entrypoint with `BlackHole 2ch` present. The run starts a real node command-pair session, pulls valid input bytes from the input child, rejects malformed `pushAudio` before output bytes are written, accepts valid `pushAudio`, and proves malformed pulled-audio responses stop the realtime bridge at the 5-error cutoff:

